.Dd September 4, 2026
.Dt LANDSTRIP 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm landstrip
.Nd run commands inside an OS-level sandbox
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -debug
.Ar command
.Op Ar options
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
runs a program under the current platform's sandbox using JSON or YAML policies
in a supported subset of the Anthropic Sandbox Runtime format.
.Pp
Run
.Nm Ar command Fl -help
for complete command options.
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width "policy validate"
.It Cm run
Run a program under the merged policy.
The
.Fl -
separator before the program is required.
.It Cm policy validate
Check policy parsing, path resolution, executable policy, and platform support.
.It Cm policy resolve
Print the normalized merged policy as JSON.
.It Cm doctor
Print a JSON report showing whether the current sandbox is usable.
.It Cm windows install
Install restricted-user isolation.
This Windows-only command requests elevation.
.It Cm windows status
Report the active Windows implementation and its health.
.It Cm windows uninstall
Remove restricted-user isolation and return to AppContainer.
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
.Ss General
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -debug
Enable debug logging.
.It Fl V , Fl -version
Print the version.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print help.
.El
.Ss Policy input
These options apply to
.Cm run , policy validate ,
and
.Cm policy resolve .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl p Ar file , Fl -policy Ar file
Read a policy file.
This option may be repeated.
Use
.Sq -
for standard input together with
.Fl -policy-format .
.It Fl -policy-format Ar format
Use
.Va json
or
.Va yaml .
.It Fl -tool Ar program
Include policy attached to the resolved executable when validating or resolving.
.El
.Ss Run descriptors
Linux and macOS only.
Descriptors must already be open, numbered 3 or higher, and distinct.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -trap Ar FD
Write structured events to one descriptor; this option cannot be repeated.
The sandboxed program never inherits it.
Linux supports runtime broker traps and approval queries; macOS reports only
setup and launch failures, closing the descriptor on successful exec.
See
.Sx OUTPUT
for the event protocol.
.It Fl -inherit Ar FD[,FD...]
Preserve a comma-separated list of descriptors across sandbox exec;
repeat to append more descriptors.
Duplicates and overlap with
.Fl -trap
are rejected.
Inherited descriptors grant access to their resources outside pathname and
network policy.
All other descriptors except standard input, output, and error are closed
before sandbox exec.
.El
.Ss Windows installation
These options apply to
.Cm windows install .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -restricted-accounts Ar count
Set the restricted-network Windows account pool size.
.It Fl -unrestricted-accounts Ar count
Set the unrestricted-network Windows account pool size.
.It Fl -proxy-port-range Ar low-high
Set the restricted-user loopback proxy range.
.El
.Sh POLICY
Policy documents and their filesystem sections must be objects.
Objects merge recursively, arrays combine without duplicates, and later scalar
values replace earlier values.
Executable policy is merged last.
.Va denyReadAlways
roots accumulate across documents, including newline-string lists;
empty lists and null values cannot remove earlier roots.
.Pp
Example fields (platform limits are described below):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
  "filesystem": {
    "allowWrite": ["."],
    "denyWrite": ["**/.env"],
    "denyRead": ["~/.ssh"],
    "denyReadAlways": ["~/.config/agent/auth.json"],
    "allowRead": ["~/.ssh/config"]
  },
  "network": {
    "allowNetwork": false,
    "httpProxyPort": 8080,
    "socksProxyPort": 8081,
    "allowLocalBinding": false,
    "allowUnixSockets": []
  },
  "windows": {
    "appContainerMode": "lpac",
    "allowLoopback": false
  }
}
.Ed
.Pp
Relative paths resolve from the starting directory; tilde paths use the current
user's home directory.
Path entries accept shell-style globs.
.Ss Filesystem
Writes are denied by default.
.Va allowWrite
grants writable roots and
.Va denyWrite
always takes precedence.
Reads default to unrestricted on Linux and macOS.
.Va allowRead
adds exceptions to
.Va denyRead
roots, but never to
.Va denyReadAlways .
These protected roots and their descendants remain denied through canonical
symlink aliases and cannot be overridden by Linux broker approval.
Use concrete paths for credentials: read globs expand at startup and do not
match future entries.
.Pp
Linux and macOS enforce concrete and glob write denials at access time.
Windows enforces only concrete paths that exist at startup, not globs.
.Ss Network
Direct TCP connections are denied by default.
On Linux and macOS, proxy ports permit host-local TCP connections but do not
start a proxy.
.Va allowLocalBinding
permits local TCP binding and connections; macOS also permits host-local UDP.
Linux denies UDP sockets in restricted mode.
macOS uses Seatbelt's localhost matcher, which may include other local
interfaces, not just loopback; remote UDP remains denied.
.Va allowUnixSockets
permits access to listed pathname sockets, denied by default.
Windows supports neither local binding nor Unix socket policy.
.Va allowNetwork
permits general network access, including remote UDP; filesystem and
platform escape-prevention rules remain active.
.Ss Windows
Windows requires an explicit read allowlist and rejects nonempty
.Va denyReadAlways
before path resolution, even for missing paths or unmatched globs.
.Va windows.appContainerMode
selects
.Va lpac
or
.Va standard .
LPAC is the default and stronger boundary.
.Va windows.allowLoopback
exposes all local loopback services in AppContainer mode, which has no
per-port proxy grants.
Restricted-user mode
instead permits only the proxy range selected during installation.
.Sh PLATFORM NOTES
.Bl -tag -width Windows
.It Linux
Landlock enforces filesystem roots; a seccomp broker handles network and
filesystem traps, denying service-manager sockets even with unrestricted
networking.
Linux 6.12 and newer also restrict abstract Unix sockets and signals to
processes outside the sandbox.
The io_uring setup, enter, and register syscalls are blocked, but an active
.Dv IORING_SETUP_SQPOLL
ring supplied by another process can consume shared-memory submissions and is
outside this boundary.
.It macOS
Seatbelt enforces a profile fixed at process startup, without runtime trap
reporting or approval queries.
.It Windows
AppContainer or restricted local users enforce explicit filesystem grants.
Restricted-user mode uses Windows Filtering Platform rules.
.El
.Sh OUTPUT
Policy, doctor, and Windows management commands write one JSON document to
standard output.
Failures and reported Linux broker denials are JSON lines on standard error
with stable kind and code fields.
Trap kinds are
.Va filesystem , network , launch , usage ,
and
.Va internal ;
filesystem and network events are Linux-only, not an exhaustive access audit.
.Pp
.Fl -trap
also receives these events, except usage errors.
Linux approval queries require a bidirectional stream socket and are sent
only there.
Reply with a JSON line containing the query's unchanged string
.Va query_id
and an
.Va action
of
.Va allow
or
.Va deny .
An unanswered query holds the sandboxed syscall.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Cm run
returns the sandboxed program's status.
When a Linux seccomp broker is active, remaining descendants are terminated
and reaped when the program exits; background processes cannot outlive the run.
Invalid policies, unhealthy doctor or status reports, and other failures
return 1;
usage errors return 2.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Run tests with a JSON policy:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
landstrip run -p policy.json -- cargo test
.Ed
.Pp
Validate YAML from standard input:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
cat policy.yaml | landstrip policy validate -p - --policy-format yaml
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Lk https://github.com/landstrip/landstrip#readme
